EXIT(3)                        P32W Unix                         EXIT(3)

NAME
       exit, atexit, abort - end the program

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdlib.h>

       void exit(int status);
       int atexit(void (*fn)(void));
       void abort(void);

DESCRIPTION
       exit ends the program with the exit status status (0 to 255;
       EXIT_SUCCESS is 0, EXIT_FAILURE 1), which the parent's
       wait(2) receives and the shell shows as $?. First it calls
       the functions registered with atexit, the last registered
       first, then flushes every stdio stream open for writing
       (fflush(NULL)), then calls _exit (exit(2)), which closes the
       files and ends the process. Returning from main is exit with
       main's result (crt0 calls exit).

       atexit registers fn to be called by exit. At most 8 functions
       can be registered.

       abort ends the program at once, as a failure: it flushes
       stdout and raises SIGABRT (raise(3)). A handler for SIGABRT
       runs; if it returns, abort restores the default action and
       raises the signal again, so the process ends all the same,
       killed by SIGABRT (the shell's $? is 134). assert(3) calls
       it.

RETURN VALUE
       exit and abort do not return. atexit returns 0, or -1 if 8
       functions are registered already.

EXAMPLES
       if (argc < 2) {
           fprintf(stderr, "usage: prog file\n");
           exit(1);
       }

SEE ALSO
       exit(2), wait(2), signal(2), sh(1), assert(3), raise(3),
       stdio(3)

BUGS
       abort leaves no core (P32W Unix writes none). Output buffered
       in streams other than stdout is lost by abort.
